WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.397
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:08.990
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:15.809
Unit eight: Advanced Cleft Structures. Today you will build complex cleft sentences to control focus.

4
00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.066
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:25.150
It was...that.

7
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.229
It was...that means an it-cleft frame.

8
00:00:29.817 --> 00:00:31.561
It was in May that we met.

9
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.675
Your turn. Say: It was...that.

10
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:38.592
It was...that.

11
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.328
What...is.

12
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:43.409
What...is means a wh-cleft frame.

13
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:45.807
What matters is honesty.

14
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:49.173
Your turn. Say: What...is.

15
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:52.918
What...is.

16
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:54.661
All...is.

17
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.230
All...is means a cleft meaning the only thing.

18
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:00.317
All I want is peace.

19
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:03.186
Your turn. Say: All...is.

20
00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:06.918
All...is.

21
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:08.986
Cleft sentence.

22
00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:12.772
Cleft sentence means a sentence divided to highlight.

23
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:14.907
A cleft sentence.

24
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:18.738
Your turn. Say: Cleft sentence.

25
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.623
Cleft sentence.

26
00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:25.733
Focus structure.

27
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:30.109
Focus structure means a pattern that creates focus.

28
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:32.194
A focus structure.

29
00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:35.761
Your turn. Say: Focus structure.

30
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.019
Focus structure.

31
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:42.377
Highlighting information.

32
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:46.486
Highlighting information means making a fact prominent.

33
00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:48.630
Highlighting information.

34
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:52.519
Your turn. Say: Highlighting information.

35
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.064
Highlighting information.

36
00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.212
Split sentence.

37
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:02.785
Split sentence means a sentence in two clauses.

38
00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:04.872
A split sentence.

39
00:02:05.483 --> 00:02:08.310
Your turn. Say: Split sentence.

40
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:12.574
Split sentence.

41
00:02:13.317 --> 00:02:14.631
Emphasis structure.

42
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:18.510
Emphasis structure means a pattern for emphasis.

43
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:20.884
An emphasis structure.

44
00:02:21.483 --> 00:02:24.565
Your turn. Say: Emphasis structure.

45
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.993
Emphasis structure.

46
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.277
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:38.682
Visal, beyond It was Tom who called, which cleft structures do advanced writers use?

48
00:02:39.783 --> 00:02:53.398
We have wh-clefts: What we need is more time. Reversed wh-clefts: More time is what we need. And All I want is peace, which means the only thing I want.

49
00:02:54.483 --> 00:02:56.042
What can be focused?

50
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:11.261
Nearly any element: a subject, an object, an adverbial, even a whole action: What she did was resign. The focus always lands at the end or just after be.

51
00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.502
Any limits?

52
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:28.440
Use clefts where the contrast is real. If every sentence is a cleft, nothing stands out. Also check agreement: What we need are facts takes a plural verb in formal usage.

53
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.042
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.883 --> 00:03:41.917
Grammar focus: Clefts: It plus be plus focus plus that-clause; What plus clause plus be plus focus; All plus clause plus be plus focus.

55
00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:43.346
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.957
It was in May that we first met.

57
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.490
What matters is honesty.

58
00:03:49.383 --> 00:03:51.267
All I want is peace and quiet.

59
00:03:52.233 --> 00:03:58.612
Use it-clefts, wh-clefts and all-clefts to place the key information in focus. Do not overuse them.

60
00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.281
Be careful! Don't say: What I want are peace.

61
00:04:04.233 --> 00:04:06.233
Say: What I want is peace.

62
00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:08.056
Use is after the cleft.

63
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:11.635
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:13.852
What Really Matters.

65
00:04:14.152 --> 00:04:16.071
What matters most is honesty.

66
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:19.682
It was in May that the project finally began.

67
00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:22.817
All they wanted was a fair chance.

68
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:27.052
What she did was resign, and that surprised everyone.

69
00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.192
Different clefts focus on different parts of a message.

70
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.383
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:37.217 --> 00:04:39.054
blank matters is honesty.

72
00:04:39.354 --> 00:04:42.102
A, What. B, That. C, Who.

73
00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.655
The answer is A: What.

74
00:04:48.817 --> 00:04:50.398
All I want blank peace.

75
00:04:50.698 --> 00:04:53.852
A, are. B, is. C, be.

76
00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.982
The answer is B: is.

77
00:05:00.117 --> 00:05:01.896
It was in May blank we met.

78
00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:05.222
A, that. B, what. C, where.

79
00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:10.894
The answer is A: that.

80
00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:15.118
The focus in a wh-cleft usually comes blank.

81
00:05:15.418 --> 00:05:19.036
A, at the end. B, first. C, never.

82
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.855
The answer is A: at the end.

83
00:05:26.017 --> 00:05:28.283
According to the reading, what did she do?

84
00:05:28.583 --> 00:05:31.819
A, resign. B, travel. C, win.

85
00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.180
The answer is A: resign.

86
00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:41.565
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:42.417 --> 00:05:46.076
Rewrite a simple sentence as a cleft sentence to add emphasis.

88
00:05:54.883 --> 00:05:56.314
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:56.614 --> 00:06:06.667
I need more time. What I need is more time. Another form is: It is more time that I need. The cleft makes my request sound stronger and more specific.

90
00:06:06.967 --> 00:06:08.688
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:09.583 --> 00:06:21.187
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and clefts: it plus be plus focus plus that-clause; what plus clause plus be plus focus; all plus clause plus be plus focus.

92
00:06:21.487 --> 00:06:26.647
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:06:26.947 --> 00:06:29.390
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.203
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

